they notice the country club across the

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.560
street, right? That's a little nicer.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:02.919
So maybe I'll work a little harder.

00:01:02.959 --> 00:01:04.120
And then they join the one across the

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:05.400
street and they work a little harder and

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:06.680
then they're like, "Oh, well maybe then I'll

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:07.720
get to this thing and that thing and

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.000
that thing." And at the end of the

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:10.520
day, the only time they see the fancy

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.120
country club is when their kids post photos

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:13.560
from the pool from Instagram because they're

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.240
so busy working so they can avoid the

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:16.840
thing that they don't actually get to enjoy.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:18.759
Right? So we're so busy trying to fulfill

00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:21.240
everybody else's definition of what success

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:22.199
will finally mean.

00:01:22.239 --> 00:01:24.600
And it's when we're so busy working towards

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.280
somebody else's definition that we lose

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.520
ourselves. And when we lose ourselves, we

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.919
don't bring our best selves to work or

00:01:30.959 --> 00:01:31.400
to home.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.240
Okay. So here's the job as an executive

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.319
recruiter. It is your job to call the

00:01:35.359 --> 00:01:36.840
most successful people in the world, right?

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.840
are hired by your client to find the

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.920
most successful person and to recruit them

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.040
away from that amazing job

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:44.840
to work for your client.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.400
Now, you're calling a super successful

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.319
person about a job they didn't know was

00:01:49.359 --> 00:01:52.000
available, maybe in a company they didn't

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.960
know existed or didn't know what new

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.280
direction or exciting strategic plan they

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.280
were following. Certainly, a recruiter they

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.640
never knew existed.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.960
So, you're starting from a pretty, you know,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.479
you're starting pretty far behind the start

00:02:03.519 --> 00:02:05.159
line. So, you're calling them because

00:02:05.199 --> 00:02:06.280
they're super successful.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:07.919
But despite all that success,

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.200
they aren't very happy and they're all

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:10.840
calling you back.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:12.639
And I was fascinated.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.400
I mean, like fascinated by the idea that

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.319
these super successful people, like on

00:02:17.359 --> 00:02:19.719
paper, that's why I'm calling them, weren't

00:02:19.759 --> 00:02:21.640
actually happy because you would think the

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:22.920
job would be really hard, right?

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.400
Call these people who like they don't know

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:25.960
who I am.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:27.159
They're happy where they are.

00:02:27.199 --> 00:02:27.680
They're not.

00:02:27.759 --> 00:02:29.560
And because I thought about what makes a

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.640
good job good, these eight motivating

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.399
factors, but not what makes a good job

00:02:33.439 --> 00:02:34.560
good for them.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:35.360
Yeah.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.279
they left and I couldn't keep them.

00:02:38.319 --> 00:02:39.959
And so throughout these 20 years of

00:02:39.999 --> 00:02:41.640
executive search, I just became so

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.719
fascinated by the fact that every single one

00:02:43.759 --> 00:02:45.320
of us actually has a very different

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:46.719
definition of success

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.520
than each other.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.279
But also at every age and at every

00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:52.920
life stage, that definition also changes.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.519
I think it starts by asking yourself whose

00:02:55.559 --> 00:02:56.759
goal is this?

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:58.720
Great. Yeah. Whose goal is this?

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.480
And the reason that I did not do

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:01.840
well in law school is it turned out

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.920
that it was my fourth grade teacher's goal.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.120
It was my parents goal.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.960
It wasn't my goal.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.120
I didn't really I walked in the very

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:10.560
first day and I looked around and I

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.800
was like I've made a huge mistake.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.400
Like I don't belong here.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:15.360
I don't want to be here.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.879
This is like what is happening?

00:03:16.919 --> 00:03:19.200
And by the way, I got horrible grades

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:20.960
because when you're not in a place that's

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.960
right for you, you don't bring the best

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.480
of you. And so we get very trapped

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.040
